# justelesdocs runtime configuration. Copy to docker/.env (gitignored) and edit.
#
#   cp docker/env.example docker/.env
#
# Every key here is optional: with no docker/.env at all the site serves with
# analytics off, the DEV banner off, and the search pointed at the default
# encoder container name. The defaults are chosen so that an unconfigured deploy
# is the SAFE one (nothing tracked, nothing exposed), never the convenient one.
#
# docker/.env does double duty, which is worth understanding before editing:
#   - compose reads it to interpolate ${VAR} in docker-compose.yml (BIND_ADDR and
#     PORT below). These shape the container.
#   - it is ALSO the service's env_file, so every key reaches the container
#     environment, where docker/entrypoint.sh and docker/Caddyfile read them.
# A key that appears in neither of those two places does nothing at all.


# =============================================================================
# THE DEPLOYMENT: names, data, networks (compose interpolation only)
# =============================================================================
# The compose project name AND the prefix of every container name (<id>,
# <id>-search, <id>-pages, <id>-embed). Two sites on one host need two ids, or
# each `up --force-recreate` replaces the other's containers. A deployment that
# predates this variable sets it to the name it already runs under, or the next
# `up` starts a second stack beside the first (and fails on the published port).
#SITE_ID=justelesdocs

# The corpus's built tree (www/, index/, restricted/), relative to docker/. The
# default is the layout deploy.sh produces on the server.
#DIST_DIR=../dist

# This stack's default network. Pinned rather than left to Docker's pool for the
# reason docker-compose.yml records; a second site on the host picks another /24.
#NETWORK_SUBNET=172.16.242.0/24

# The external network the search service reaches the encoder over. It must
# exist before `up` (sudo docker network create <name>).
#EMBED_NETWORK=justeles-embed

# OPTIONAL encoder in this stack (`docker compose --profile embed up`), for a host
# with no justelesRCP running: built from a justelesRCP checkout with that repo's
# own Dockerfile, query-only. Set EMBED_URL=http://embed:8461 below to use it.
#EMBED_CONTEXT=../../justelesRCP
#EMBED_MODELS=../../justelesRCP/models
#EMBED_MEM_LIMIT=2g


# =============================================================================
# THE SEARCH SERVICE
# =============================================================================
# Ranking runs in the `search` container (server/service.mjs), not in the
# browser: the page posts the question to /api/search and Caddy reverse-proxies
# it there over the compose network, by SERVICE NAME, through Docker's embedded
# DNS. Nothing is published on the host. Override only if the service is renamed
# or made to listen on another port.
#SEARCH_UPSTREAM=search:8650
# Where Caddy finds the page service. Same rule as SEARCH_UPSTREAM: the service
# name on the compose network, never published on the host.
#PAGES_UPSTREAM=pages:8651

# That service, in turn, asks an encoder for the query vector, and this project
# ships no encoder. The VPS is small and the model is around 1 GB resident, so
# exactly one copy of it may be loaded, and it is justelesRCP's (see DESIGN.md,
# "Embedding service").
#
# The two projects meet on a shared Docker network called `justeles-embed`. Both
# compose files declare it `external: true`, so neither creates or deletes it,
# and it has to exist before either stack starts. Both deploy.sh create it, but a
# first deploy by hand needs it once:
#
#   sudo docker network create --subnet 172.16.240.0/24 justeles-embed
#
# The --subnet is required, not a preference. An unpinned bridge takes whatever
# Docker's default pool has left, and on a host that has used up the pool's first
# range it lands in the 192.168 fallback, where it can end up with no outbound
# route at all while every 172.x bridge on the same host works.
#
# On that network the encoder answers to its CONTAINER NAME, justelesrcp-embed,
# and nothing is published on the host by either side. Two things that look like
# they should work and do not, recorded because both cost an evening: a port
# published on the host's 127.0.0.1 is unreachable from a container (the
# connection arrives from the bridge subnet, not from loopback), and compose's
# `ports:` accepts an IP and never a hostname, so a bind address of
# host.docker.internal is rejected before the stack even starts.
#
# Check it from inside the search container, which is the only place the answer
# means anything:
#   sudo docker exec <SITE_ID>-search wget -qO- http://justelesrcp-embed:8461/api/sem/health


# =============================================================================
# SEARCH
# =============================================================================
# Ranking runs in the `search` container (server/service.mjs), which reads every
# key below from this file through compose's env_file. The page never sees them:
# it posts the question to /api/search and shows what comes back.

# MUST match the width the passage index was baked and truncated to, which
# dist/index/meta.json records (`dims`) and build_index.py --dims sets. The
# service refuses to START when the two disagree, with the message
#   index is 1024 dims, SEARCH_DIM asks for 256
# and the container restarts until the deploy fixes one side. Left unset, the
# service trusts whatever width the index declares. Set it anyway: the one
# deploy that shipped a 256-dim index against a 1024-dim configuration is the
# reason the check exists, and a refusal at startup is the failure you want.
SEARCH_DIM=1024

# The cosine below which a hit is hidden. The service applies it once, when it
# folds the candidate list, and every /api/search answer carries the value it
# used so the page can say "nothing above the floor" honestly.
#
# Calibrate it from the measurement, not from intuition. Re-measured on
# 2026-09-26 against the SHIPPED index (jina-embeddings-v5, 256-token chunks,
# 128,841 rows) through the shipped ranker, by evaluate_rescore.mjs over the 200
# eval queries plus seven questions this corpus does not answer. A correct hit
# scores 0.314 at worst, 0.516 at the median and 0.644 at best, so the "0.55 to
# 0.70" of the first read-by-eye pass was optimistic and a floor set from it
# would have hidden half the right answers.
#
# 0.28 is where it lands: no correct hit is below it (the worst is 0.314), and
# every one of the five genuinely off-topic questions tops out under it (apple
# tart 0.216, timing belt 0.242, train fares 0.245, petanque 0.264, programming
# languages 0.267). Quadrupling the index from 30,746 rows did not erode that;
# the best off-corpus score went DOWN, 0.299 to 0.267. 0 disables it.
#
# What it cannot do, and should not be tuned to try: separate adjacent medicine
# from this corpus. Chronic heart failure scores 0.461 here and seasonal flu
# vaccination 0.425, both above the median correct hit, and refusing them would
# mean a floor of 0.45 that hides 34 correct hits and empties 26 queries.
#
# This replaces an UNMEASURED ON THE CURRENT ENCODER warning that told whoever
# read it to keep the live docker/.env at 0 until the re-measurement happened.
# It has happened, and the live value moved from 0.0 to 0.28 with it.
SEARCH_FLOOR=0.28

# How many searches may wait behind the one being ranked. Ranking is serial
# (one worker: the index is one buffer and a second worker would halve its
# cache hits), so under a burst the queue is what keeps the container
# responsive: the 25th concurrent search is refused with 503 and Retry-After
# instead of everyone waiting a second longer. Raise it only after measuring
# the VPS; the per-IP limit below is the first line of defence.
# The same number bounds every search in flight, the encoder round trip
# included: at most depth + 1 at once. It must be a positive integer, and
# the service refuses to start otherwise.
#SEARCH_QUEUE_DEPTH=24

# How many documents' text the service keeps resident for the BM25 pass and
# the result snippets. Each is that document's full text, 50 KB median and up
# to 6 MB, so this is the knob that decides the container's memory: about
# 70 MB resident with the index alone, plus this many documents' worth.
# Oldest out beyond the cap.
# Measured (DESIGN.md): 16 saves nothing visible and 0 costs about 120 ms per
# answer, so 64 it stays. The container's memory is bounded by the heap cap in
# search.Dockerfile, not by this.
#SEARCH_TEXT_CACHE=64

# Where the service asks for query vectors: the sibling's encoder, by CONTAINER
# NAME over the justeles-embed network (see THE SEARCH SERVICE above). Same
# default in server/service.mjs. Override only if that container is renamed or
# moves to another host.
#EMBED_URL=http://justelesrcp-embed:8461

# The uid:gid ALL THREE containers run as (web, search, pages). Same convention
# as the sibling's services: the bind mounts are host-owned, so each container
# reads them as the host user, never as root. Set both to the deploying user's
# ids on the VPS (`id -u`, `id -g`). Named SEARCH_UID/SEARCH_GID until
# 2026-10-03, when Caddy stopped running as root too; the old names are still
# read as a fallback, so an older docker/.env keeps working, but set these.
#APP_UID=1000
#APP_GID=1000

# Per-IP rate limit on /api/search and /api/search/health (caddy-ratelimit).
# The default allows 60 requests per minute per IP: a search session sends one
# debounced search per keystroke pause, so a request a second is far above any
# human pace and still stops a crude flood. Worth keeping tight-ish: the service
# ranks on one worker behind a bounded queue, so a flood from one visitor would
# answer 503 to every other, and every query it accepts goes on to the SHARED
# encoder, so abuse against this site degrades justelesRCP as well.
#SEARCH_RATE_EVENTS=60
#SEARCH_RATE_WINDOW=1m

# Per-IP rate limit on /pdf/* (caddy-ratelimit), the corpus documents themselves.
# These are the only large responses the site serves, so this is the bandwidth
# knob rather than the CPU one. 300 per minute is deliberately loose: pdf.js
# fetches a PDF by HTTP Range, so opening a single page costs several requests
# and a reader comparing four documents can legitimately spend a few dozen. It
# still caps a scrape of the whole corpus at a pace the VPS can absorb. Lower it
# if bandwidth is the problem; do not lower it below about 60 or normal reading
# starts failing mid-document, which looks like a corrupt PDF rather than a
# refusal.
#PDF_RATE_EVENTS=300
#PDF_RATE_WINDOW=1m

# Per-IP rate limit on /api/page (caddy-ratelimit), which is how the 53
# RESTRICTED documents are read: one request returns one page, cut out of the
# document, and the document itself is not reachable at any URL. This limit is
# therefore doing something the others are not. It is the only thing standing
# between a reader looking at a page and somebody walking a 1,275-page book one
# request at a time, so it is the knob to tighten if that ever shows up in the
# logs. It still has to clear normal reading: a reader who pages through a
# document spends one request per page, and the viewer prefetches the page
# either side of a hit, so 120 a minute is about four times a comfortable
# reading pace. Below about 30 and paging starts failing in front of the reader.
#PAGE_RATE_EVENTS=120
#PAGE_RATE_WINDOW=1m

# How many page cuts may run at once, and how many opened documents the service
# keeps. A cut is milliseconds of CPU (4 ms for page 500 of a 1,275-page book, measured)
# but it is CPU this box shares with the ranker and the sibling's encoder, so the
# default is two. The cache holds mupdf's page tree per document, which is what
# makes paging through one document cheap after the first page; each entry costs
# a file handle and some memory, hence the bound.
#PAGES_CONCURRENCY=2
#PAGES_CACHE=4

# =============================================================================
# NETWORK EXPOSURE
# =============================================================================
# Host port. 8648 by default, chosen because it collides with nothing else the
# owner runs on that host.
#PORT=8648

# Host interface to bind, INCLUDING the trailing colon, or empty for all
# interfaces. Defaults to loopback only, which is correct when TLS is terminated
# by a reverse proxy on the same host. Set it empty ONLY if the proxy runs
# elsewhere and must reach this over the network, and understand that the site is
# then served in plain HTTP to anything that can route to the host.
#BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1:
#BIND_ADDR=

# CIDR or address of the upstream TLS proxy, for recovering the visitor's IP from
# X-Forwarded-For. Defaults to `private_ranges` (loopback plus RFC1918), which is
# right when the proxy is local. If the proxy sits on a PUBLIC IP, set it here:
# otherwise every request appears to come from the proxy and the per-IP rate
# limit collapses into one global bucket.
#TRUSTED_PROXIES=203.0.113.7/32


# =============================================================================
# ANALYTICS (privacy-friendly umami, entirely optional)
# =============================================================================
# Point this at the umami TRACKER SCRIPT, not the umami instance's base URL. The
# entrypoint validates it at startup and refuses to boot if it is unreachable or
# does not return JavaScript, because the failure it is guarding against is
# silent: a base URL is reachable, returns HTML, and records zero events while
# looking perfectly configured. Leave it unset to disable analytics entirely, in
# which case no third-party origin appears in the CSP at all.
#
# What is actually sent, decided in src/site.js: ONE page view per page load,
# carrying location.pathname and nothing else. umami's own auto-tracking is turned
# off on purpose, because a result list lives at /?q=<the question> and a question
# asked of a medical or legal corpus must not reach an analytics database. No click or
# event tracking is wired at all.
#ANALYTICS_URL=https://umami.example.org/script.js
#ANALYTICS_WEBSITE_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Two variables reach the container WITHOUT appearing in this file, and grepping
# for them here is how you conclude, wrongly, that they are unplumbed:
#   ANALYTICS_ORIGIN  derived by the entrypoint from ANALYTICS_URL's scheme and
#                     host, and exported for docker/Caddyfile, which needs the
#                     ORIGIN (not the script URL) in script-src and connect-src.
#                     Deriving it means the CSP cannot disagree with the script
#                     the page actually loads, which is a failure that shows up
#                     only as silently zero analytics.
#   STARTED_AT        set by the entrypoint to the container's boot time, for the
#                     DEV banner's "restarted X ago" and for the localStorage key
#                     that scopes its dismissal to one build.
# Setting either one here does nothing: the entrypoint overwrites both.

# Subresource-integrity hash for the tracker script, if your instance publishes
# one. Empty means the script loads without an integrity check.
#ANALYTICS_SRI=

# Honour Do-Not-Track. Defaults to on (true) in the client when empty: the
# default is the privacy-respecting one, and switching it off is the deliberate
# act that needs a line in this file.
#ANALYTICS_DNT=true


# =============================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT
# =============================================================================
# Any non-empty value shows the DEV banner (build identity, "restarted X ago").
# Leave unset in production.
#DEV=

# Where "the code of this site is on GitHub" points, in the footer and in the
# development banner. Left empty the footer still links the repository, at its public
# URL: set this only when serving a fork, or a mirror, or a private copy.
#SOURCE_URL=https://github.com/OWNER/justelesdocs
